@chapter Stack Backend
@node Stack Backend
@section Stack Backend

The stack backend (also called the Forth backend) is a virtual machine used to execute
Fountain scripts in the Phantasia game engine. This virtual machine is a 16-bit stack
machine similar to Forth, and is implemented in @file{Source/Code/7800/Routines/Forth.s}.

The machine operates on a parameter stack holding 16-bit values. The stack grows
downward in memory, and the stack pointer indicates the next free slot.

@menu
* Opcode Reference::
* Stack Layout::
* Interpreter Implementation::
* ABI and Compilation::
@end menu

@node Opcode Reference
@subsection Opcode Reference

The following table lists all valid opcodes (bytecodes) recognized by the stack backend,
their hexadecimal values, and their effect on the parameter stack. All multi-byte
operands are little-endian (low byte first).

@table @asis
@item ForthEOL ($00) - End of Script
Stack effect: @dots{} @rightarrow{} @dots{}
Description: Signals the end of the script. Not a traditional opcode; the interpreter
checks this and returns from the script. No stack effect.

@item ForthPushWord ($01) - Push 16-bit Immediate
Stack effect: @dots{} @rightarrow{} @dots{}, @var{value}
Description: Reads two bytes from the instruction stream (little-endian) and pushes
them as a 16-bit value. The instruction pointer advances by 2 bytes.

@item ForthSetByte ($02) - Store Byte
Stack effect: @var{value}, @var{address} @rightarrow{} @dots{}
Description: Pops an address and a value (16-bit, but only the low byte is stored).
Stores the low byte of @var{value} at the 16-bit address @var{address}.
Corresponds to the 6502 @code{STA} instruction.

@item ForthSetWord ($03) - Store 16-bit Word
Stack effect: @var{value}, @var{address} @rightarrow{} @dots{}
Description: Pops an address and a value. Stores the 16-bit @var{value} at the 16-bit
address @var{address} in little-endian order. Corresponds to 6502 @code{STA} twice.

@item ForthGetByte ($04) - Fetch Byte
Stack effect: @var{address} @rightarrow{} @dots{}, @var{value}
Description: Replaces the address on the stack with the byte read from that address,
zero-extended to 16-bit (value 0-255). Corresponds to 6502 @code{LDA} with zero-extend.

@item ForthGetWord ($05) - Fetch 16-bit Word
Stack effect: @var{address} @rightarrow{} @dots{}, @var{value}
Description: Replaces the address on the stack with the 16-bit value read from that
address (little-endian). Corresponds to two @code{LDA} instructions.

@item ForthExecute ($06) - Call Subroutine
Stack effect: @var{address} @rightarrow{} @dots{}
Description: Pops the address and jumps to it. The stack is popped by two bytes (one
16-bit item) before the jump. Used for subroutine calls. The target address should
typically be in the $8000-$BFFF range (RAM or low ROM) on the 7800.

@item ForthDup ($07) - Duplicate Top of Stack
Stack effect: @var{x} @rightarrow{} @dots{}, @var{x}, @var{x}
Description: Pushes a copy of the top item. Requires at least one item on stack.

@item ForthDrop ($08) - Discard Top of Stack
Stack effect: @var{x} @rightarrow{} @dots{}
Description: Removes the top item from the stack. Requires at least one item on stack.

@item ForthUnless ($09) - Conditional Branch on False
Stack effect: @var{condition} @rightarrow{} @dots{}
Description: Pops a 16-bit condition. If the condition is zero, the instruction pointer
is advanced by two bytes (skipping a 16-bit offset operand). Otherwise, execution
continates normally. Used for IF...ELSE constructs.

@item ForthWhen ($0a) - Conditional Branch on True
Stack effect: @var{condition} @rightarrow{} @dots{}
Description: Pops a 16-bit condition. If the condition is non-zero, execution continues
normally. If the condition is zero, the instruction pointer advances by two bytes
(skipping a 16-bit offset operand). Used for IF...THEN constructs.

@item ForthGo ($0b) - Unconditional Jump
Stack effect: @dots{} @rightarrow{} @dots{}
Description: Reads two bytes from the instruction stream (little-endian) and sets the
instruction pointer to that address. Does not affect the stack.

@item ForthSwap ($0c) - Swap Top Two Items
Stack effect: @var{x}, @var{y} @rightarrow{} @dots{}, @var{y}, @var{x}
Description: Exchanges the top two items on the stack. Requires at least two items.
Corresponds to rotating the top two stack entries.

@item ForthPushByte ($0d) - Push 8-bit Immediate
Stack effect: @dots{} @rightarrow{} @dots{}, @var{value}
Description: Reads one byte from the instruction stream and pushes it as a 16-bit
value with the high byte zero (zero-extended). Useful for small constants (0-255)
to save bytecode space.
@end table

@node Stack Layout
@subsection Stack Layout and Memory Model

The parameter stack is implemented as a region in RAM, with the stack pointer
(@code{ForthStack}) indicating the next free slot. The stack grows downward in memory.

@subsubheading Memory Layout

@example
Stack Memory Base: $XXXX (configurable, typically in RAM)
Stack Pointer (SP):  $XX  (0-255, index into stack memory)

Stack grows downward:
  [SP+0, SP+1] = top of stack (16-bit, low byte first)
  [SP+2, SP+3] = second item
  [SP+4, SP+5] = third item
  ...
@end example

On the Atari 7800, the stack is implemented as a page ($100 bytes) in zero page
for fast access. The stack pointer starts at $81 (leaving one slot as a safety
margin).

@subsubheading Stack Pointer Conventions

@itemize @bullet
@item Stack pointer points to the next free (empty) slot
@item Pushing decrements SP by 2
@item Popping increments SP by 2
@item Stack underflow is detected when SP reaches $80 (one slot above the guard)
@item Stack overflow is detected when SP reaches $02 (no room for another item)
@end itemize

@subsubheading Zero-Page Variables (7800 Implementation)

@example
ForthStack     = $80   ; Stack pointer (zero page)
ForthCursor    = $82   ; Instruction pointer (2 bytes)
StackTop       = $00   ; Assumes X points to ForthStack
StackNext      = $02   ; One word below top
StackSecond    = $04   ; Two words below top
ForthStackOneArg = $82 ; Minimum SP for one argument
ForthStackTwoArgs = $84 ; Minimum SP for two arguments
@end example

@node Interpreter Implementation
@subsection Interpreter Implementation

This section provides complete interpreter implementations for several platforms.
The interpreter follows a fetch-decode-execute cycle.

@subsubheading Common Design Patterns

All interpreters share these characteristics:
@itemize @bullet
@item Instruction pointer points to the next byte to execute
@item Dispatch table maps opcode to handler routine
@item Handlers operate on the stack using push/pop operations
@item Branch offsets are relative to the instruction pointer after reading the offset
@end itemize

@subsubheading 6502 Assembly (7800 Atari)

This is the reference implementation from @file{Source/Code/7800/Routines/Forth.s}:

@example
;; ForthInterpreter - Main fetch-decode-execute loop
;; Uses zero-page for fast stack access
;; Assumes: ForthStack at $80, ForthCursor at $82

ForthInterpreter:
        lda ForthCursor+1        ; Check if cursor high byte is 0
        bne Stay

Return:
        jmp ScriptFinis          ; Exit interpreter

Stay:
        ldx ForthStack
        cpx #$82                  ; Check for underflow
        blt StackUnderflow

InStack:
        jsr ForthExecuteOneOpcode ; Execute one instruction
        jmp ForthInterpreter     ; Loop

;; Execute single opcode
ForthExecuteOneOpcode:
        ldy #0
        lda (ForthCursor),y       ; Fetch opcode
        sta LastForthOp
        inc ForthCursor           ; Advance cursor
        bne +
        inc ForthCursor+1
+
        tay                       ; Opcode to Y register

        ;; Dispatch table (simplified)
        cpy #ForthEOL
        beq Done
        cpy #ForthPushWord
        beq ForthPushOp
        cpy #ForthPushByte
        beq ForthPushByteOp
        cpy #ForthSetByte
        beq ForthSetByteOp
        ... (more cases)

;; PushWord: Push 16-bit immediate
;; Format: $01 low high
ForthPushOp:
        ldx ForthStack
        ldy #0
        lda (ForthCursor),y       ; Low byte
        sta StackNext,x           ; Store at SP
        iny
        lda (ForthCursor),y       ; High byte
        sta StackNext+1,x
        dec ForthStack            ; Decrement SP
        dec ForthStack
        .Add16 ForthCursor,#2      ; Advance cursor
        rts

;; PushByte: Push 8-bit immediate (zero-extended)
;; Format: $0D value
ForthPushByteOp:
        ldy #0
        ldx ForthStack
        lda (ForthCursor),y       ; Value
        sta StackNext,x           ; Low byte
        iny
        lda #0
        sta StackNext+1,x         ; Zero-extend
        dec ForthStack
        dec ForthStack
        .Inc16 ForthCursor
        rts

;; SetWord: Store 16-bit word at address
;; Stack: address, value (value on top)
ForthSetWordOp:
        ldx ForthStack
        lda StackTop,x            ; Low byte of value
        sta (StackSecond,x)       ; Store at address (low)
        lda StackTop+1,x          ; High byte
        sta (StackSecond,x)+1     ; Store at address+1 (high)
        inc ForthStack
        inc ForthStack            ; Pop address
        rts

;; GetWord: Fetch 16-bit word from address
;; Stack: address (on top)
ForthGetWordOp:
        ldx ForthStack
        lda (StackTop,x)          ; Fetch low byte
        sta StackTop,x            ; Store as new top
        lda (StackTop,x)+1        ; Fetch high byte
        sta StackTop+1,x          ; Store high
        rts

;; Execute: Call subroutine at address
;; Stack: address (on top)
ForthExecuteOp:
        ldx ForthStack
        lda StackTop,x
        sta ThreadedVector
        lda StackTop+1,x
        sta ThreadedVector+1
        inc ForthStack
        inc ForthStack            ; Pop address
        jmp (ThreadedVector)      ; Jump to subroutine

;; Unless: Branch if zero
;; Stack: condition
ForthUnlessOp:
        ldx ForthStack
        lda StackTop,x            ; Check low byte
        bne Fail                   ; Non-zero, don't branch
        lda StackTop+1,x           ; Check high byte
        beq UnlessToGo            ; Both zero, branch

Fail:
        inc ForthStack
        inc ForthStack
        .Add16 ForthCursor,#2      ; Skip offset
        rts

UnlessToGo:
        inc ForthStack
        inc ForthStack
        jmp ForthGoOp             ; Do the jump

;; Go: Unconditional jump
;; Format: $0B low high
ForthGoOp:
        ldy #0
        lda (ForthCursor),y       ; Low byte
        ldx ForthCursor
        iny
        lda (ForthCursor),y       ; High byte
        sta ForthCursor+1
        stx ForthCursor           ; Set instruction pointer
        rts

;; When: Branch if non-zero
;; Stack: condition
ForthWhenOp:
        ldx ForthStack
        lda StackTop,x
        bne WhenToGo              ; Non-zero, take branch
        lda StackTop+1,x
        bne WhenToGo

        inc ForthStack
        inc ForthStack
        .Add16 ForthCursor,#2      ; Skip offset
        rts

WhenToGo:
        inc ForthStack
        inc ForthStack
        rts                        ; Continue to next instruction
@end example

@subsubheading C Implementation

@example
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

/* Opcodes */
#define FORTH_EOL       0x00
#define FORTH_PUSH_WORD 0x01
#define FORTH_SET_BYTE  0x02
#define FORTH_SET_WORD  0x03
#define FORTH_GET_BYTE  0x04
#define FORTH_GET_WORD  0x05
#define FORTH_EXECUTE   0x06
#define FORTH_DUP       0x07
#define FORTH_DROP      0x08
#define FORTH_UNLESS    0x09
#define FORTH_WHEN      0x0A
#define FORTH_GO        0x0B
#define FORTH_SWAP      0x0C
#define FORTH_PUSH_BYTE 0x0D

/* Stack configuration */
#define STACK_SIZE 64          /* Number of 16-bit items */
#define STACK_BASE 0x0000      /* Adjust for your platform */
#define SP_MIN    2            /* Minimum SP (overflow guard) */
#define SP_MAX    0x80         /* Maximum SP (underflow guard) */

/* VM state */
static uint16_t stack[STACK_SIZE];
static uint8_t  sp = STACK_SIZE;  /* Start full, pointing to next free */
static uint8_t  ip = 0;           /* Instruction pointer (byte index) */
static uint8_t  memory[256];       /* Forth bytecode buffer */

/* Stack operations */
static inline void push(uint16_t value) {
    if (sp <= SP_MIN) {
        /* Stack overflow - handle error */
        return;
    }
    sp -= 2;
    stack[sp / 2] = value;
}

static inline uint16_t pop(void) {
    if (sp >= STACK_SIZE) {
        /* Stack underflow - handle error */
        return 0;
    }
    uint16_t value = stack[sp / 2];
    sp += 2;
    return value;
}

/* Memory access */
static inline uint8_t read_byte(uint16_t addr) {
    return memory[addr];  /* Adjust for actual memory read */
}

static inline uint16_t read_word(uint16_t addr) {
    return read_byte(addr) | (read_byte(addr + 1) << 8);
}

static inline void write_byte(uint16_t addr, uint8_t value) {
    memory[addr] = value;  /* Adjust for actual memory write */
}

static inline void write_word(uint16_t addr, uint16_t value) {
    write_byte(addr, value & 0xFF);
    write_byte(addr + 1, value >> 8);
}

/* Fetch-decode-execute */
void forth_run(void) {
    while (1) {
        uint8_t opcode = read_byte(ip++);

        switch (opcode) {
        case FORTH_EOL:
            return;  /* Exit interpreter */

        case FORTH_PUSH_WORD: {
            uint16_t value = read_word(ip);
            ip += 2;
            push(value);
            break;
        }

        case FORTH_PUSH_BYTE: {
            uint8_t value = read_byte(ip++);
            push((uint16_t)value);
            break;
        }

        case FORTH_DUP: {
            uint16_t top = pop();
            push(top);
            push(top);
            break;
        }

        case FORTH_DROP: {
            pop();
            break;
        }

        case FORTH_SWAP: {
            uint16_t a = pop();
            uint16_t b = pop();
            push(a);
            push(b);
            break;
        }

        case FORTH_GET_BYTE: {
            uint16_t addr = pop();
            push((uint16_t)read_byte(addr));
            break;
        }

        case FORTH_GET_WORD: {
            uint16_t addr = pop();
            push(read_word(addr));
            break;
        }

        case FORTH_SET_BYTE: {
            uint16_t value = pop();
            uint16_t addr = pop();
            write_byte(addr, (uint8_t)value);
            break;
        }

        case FORTH_SET_WORD: {
            uint16_t value = pop();
            uint16_t addr = pop();
            write_word(addr, value);
            break;
        }

        case FORTH_EXECUTE: {
            uint16_t addr = pop();
            ip = (uint8_t)addr;  /* Save ip, set new ip */
            break;  /* Note: real impl needs call stack */
        }

        case FORTH_GO: {
            uint16_t addr = read_word(ip);
            ip = (uint8_t)addr;
            break;
        }

        case FORTH_WHEN: {
            uint16_t offset = read_word(ip);
            uint16_t cond = pop();
            if (cond == 0) {
                ip += offset;  /* Skip to else clause */
            }
            break;
        }

        case FORTH_UNLESS: {
            uint16_t offset = read_word(ip);
            uint16_t cond = pop();
            if (cond != 0) {
                ip += offset;  /* Skip to then clause */
            }
            break;
        }

        default:
            /* Unknown opcode - fault handler */
            return;
        }
    }
}
@end example

@subsubheading Java Implementation

@example
public class ForthVM {
    /* Opcodes */
    public static final int FORTH_EOL       = 0x00;
    public static final int FORTH_PUSH_WORD = 0x01;
    public static final int FORTH_SET_BYTE  = 0x02;
    public static final int FORTH_SET_WORD  = 0x03;
    public static final int FORTH_GET_BYTE  = 0x04;
    public static final int FORTH_GET_WORD  = 0x05;
    public static final int FORTH_EXECUTE   = 0x06;
    public static final int FORTH_DUP       = 0x07;
    public static final int FORTH_DROP      = 0x08;
    public static final int FORTH_UNLESS    = 0x09;
    public static final int FORTH_WHEN      = 0x0A;
    public static final int FORTH_GO        = 0x0B;
    public static final int FORTH_SWAP      = 0x0C;
    public static final int FORTH_PUSH_BYTE = 0x0D;

    private int[] stack = new int[64];
    private int sp = 64;  // Start at top, grow down
    private byte[] memory;
    private int ip = 0;

    public ForthVM(byte[] bytecode) {
        this.memory = bytecode;
    }

    private void push(int value) {
        stack[--sp] = value;
    }

    private int pop() {
        return stack[sp++];
    }

    private int readByte() {
        return memory[ip++] & 0xFF;
    }

    private int readWord() {
        int low = readByte();
        int high = readByte();
        return low | (high << 8);
    }

    public void run() {
        while (true) {
            int opcode = readByte();

            switch (opcode) {
            case FORTH_EOL:
                return;

            case FORTH_PUSH_WORD:
                push(readWord());
                break;

            case FORTH_PUSH_BYTE:
                push(readByte());
                break;

            case FORTH_DUP: {
                int top = pop();
                push(top);
                push(top);
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_DROP:
                pop();
                break;

            case FORTH_SWAP: {
                int a = pop();
                int b = pop();
                push(a);
                push(b);
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_GET_BYTE:
                push(memory[pop()] & 0xFF);
                break;

            case FORTH_GET_WORD: {
                int addr = pop();
                int low = memory[addr] & 0xFF;
                int high = memory[addr + 1] & 0xFF;
                push(low | (high << 8));
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_SET_BYTE: {
                int value = pop();
                int addr = pop();
                memory[addr] = (byte) value;
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_SET_WORD: {
                int value = pop();
                int addr = pop();
                memory[addr] = (byte) (value & 0xFF);
                memory[addr + 1] = (byte) (value >> 8);
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_EXECUTE: {
                int addr = pop();
                ip = addr;
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_GO:
                ip = readWord();
                break;

            case FORTH_WHEN: {
                int offset = readWord();
                if (pop() == 0) {
                    ip += offset;
                }
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_UNLESS: {
                int offset = readWord();
                if (pop() != 0) {
                    ip += offset;
                }
                break;
            }

            default:
                throw new IllegalStateException(
                    "Unknown opcode: " + Integer.toHexString(opcode));
            }
        }
    }
}
@end example

@subsubheading C++ Implementation (Object-Oriented)

@example
#include <cstdint>
#include <vector>
#include <stdexcept>

class ForthVM {
public:
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_EOL       = 0x00;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_PUSH_WORD = 0x01;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_SET_BYTE  = 0x02;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_SET_WORD  = 0x03;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_GET_BYTE  = 0x04;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_GET_WORD  = 0x05;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_EXECUTE   = 0x06;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_DUP       = 0x07;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_DROP      = 0x08;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_UNLESS    = 0x09;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_WHEN      = 0x0A;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_GO        = 0x0B;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_SWAP      = 0x0C;
    static constexpr uint8_t FORTH_PUSH_BYTE = 0x0D;

    ForthVM(const std::vector<uint8_t>& bytecode)
        : memory_(bytecode), ip_(0), sp_(stack_.size()) {}

    void run() {
        while (true) {
            uint8_t opcode = readByte();

            switch (opcode) {
            case FORTH_EOL:
                return;

            case FORTH_PUSH_WORD:
                push(readWord());
                break;

            case FORTH_PUSH_BYTE:
                push(static_cast<int16_t>(readByte()));
                break;

            case FORTH_DUP: {
                auto top = pop();
                push(top);
                push(top);
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_DROP:
                pop();
                break;

            case FORTH_SWAP: {
                auto a = pop();
                auto b = pop();
                push(a);
                push(b);
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_GET_BYTE:
                push(memory_[pop()] & 0xFF);
                break;

            case FORTH_GET_WORD: {
                auto addr = pop();
                push(readWordAt(addr));
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_SET_BYTE: {
                auto value = pop();
                auto addr = pop();
                memory_[addr] = static_cast<uint8_t>(value);
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_SET_WORD: {
                auto value = pop();
                auto addr = pop();
                memory_[addr] = static_cast<uint8_t>(value & 0xFF);
                memory_[addr + 1] = static_cast<uint8_t>(value >> 8);
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_EXECUTE:
                ip_ = pop();
                break;

            case FORTH_GO:
                ip_ = readWord();
                break;

            case FORTH_WHEN: {
                auto offset = readWord();
                if (pop() == 0) {
                    ip_ += offset;
                }
                break;
            }

            case FORTH_UNLESS: {
                auto offset = readWord();
                if (pop() != 0) {
                    ip_ += offset;
                }
                break;
            }

            default:
                throw std::runtime_error(
                    "Unknown opcode: 0x" + std::to_string(opcode));
            }
        }
    }

private:
    std::vector<int16_t> stack_{64};
    std::vector<uint8_t> memory_;
    size_t ip_ = 0;
    size_t sp_ = 0;

    void push(int16_t value) {
        stack_[--sp_] = value;
    }

    int16_t pop() {
        return stack_[sp_++];
    }

    uint8_t readByte() {
        return memory_[ip_++];
    }

    uint16_t readWord() {
        uint16_t low = readByte();
        uint16_t high = readByte();
        return low | (high << 8);
    }

    uint16_t readWordAt(size_t addr) {
        return memory_[addr] | (memory_[addr + 1] << 8);
    }
};
@end example

@subsubheading Handling Underflow and Overflow

Stack underflow and overflow must be handled carefully:

@example
/* Underflow detection */
void checkUnderflow(uint8_t needed) {
    if (sp >= STACK_SIZE - needed) {
        logFault("Stack underflow");
        sp = STACK_SIZE;  /* Reset to known state */
    }
}

/* Overflow detection */
void checkOverflow(void) {
    if (sp <= 2) {  /* Need room for at least one more */
        debugBreak("Stack overflow");
        /* Or: grow the stack, or terminate script */
    }
}
@end example

@node ABI and Compilation
@subsection ABI and Compilation Details

This section describes how Eightbol compiles COBOL or other source code to Forth
bytecode, and how the bytecode integrates with the host system.

@subsubheading Compilation Pipeline

@example
Source Code (COBOL, etc.)
        |
        v
Frontend Parser --> AST (Abstract Syntax Tree)
        |
        v
Eightbol Compiler (backend-forth)
        |
        v
Forth Bytecode (.byte sequences)
        |
        v
Forth Interpreter (Forth.s)
@end example

@subsubheading Bytecode Generation

The backend emits raw bytes in a format suitable for the target platform. On the
Atari 7800, this is assembled using the 6502 cross-assembler:

@example
; MethodDialogueShow:
; Generated by Eightbol for Stack Machine
; DO NOT EDIT -- regenerate from DialogueShow.cob

MethodDialogueShow:
        .byte #x01, #x00, #x5A    ; PUSHWORD $5A00 (DialogueIndex)
        .byte #x06                ; EXECUTE (call ShowDialogue)
        .byte #x00                ; EOL (end of method)
@end example

@subsubheading Method Calling Convention

Methods are called using the @code{ForthExecute} opcode:

@enumerate
@item Caller pushes the address of the target method onto the stack
@item Caller executes @code{ForthExecute}
@item The interpreter jumps to the method's bytecode
@item The method executes until it hits @code{ForthEOL}
@item Control returns to the instruction after @code{ForthExecute}
@end enumerate

Example call sequence:

@example
; INVOKE Dialogue.Show
        .byte #x01, <MethodDialogueShow  ; Push method address (low)
               #x01, >MethodDialogueShow ; Push method address (high)
        .byte #x06                       ; EXECUTE
; Return here after method completes
@end example

@subsubheading Variable Access

Variables are typically accessed via memory-mapped locations or global addresses:

@example
; SET Score TO 100
; Generates:
        .byte #x01, <Score           ; Push address of Score
        .byte #x04                    ; GETWORD (fetch current value)
        .byte #x01, #x00, #x64        ; PUSHWORD 100
        .byte #x0C                    ; SWAP (value, old-score)
        .byte #x01, <Score            ; Push address again
        .byte #x02                    ; SETBYTE (store low byte)
@end example

@subsubheading Conditional Flow

Conditionals use @code{ForthWhen} and @code{ForthUnless} with 16-bit relative offsets:

@example
; IF Health == 0 THEN GameOver ELSE Continue ENDIF
; Generates:
        .byte #x01, <Health           ; Push Health address
        .byte #x05                    ; GETWORD
        .byte #x01, #x00, #x00        ; PUSHWORD 0 (comparison value)
        ; ... comparison logic ...
        .byte #x0A                    ; WHEN (branch if true)
        .byte #x00, #x06              ; Offset: skip 6 bytes
        ; (GameOver code here)
        .byte #x0B, #x00, #x04        ; GO (skip else clause)
        ; (Continue code here)
        .byte #x00                    ; EOL
@end example

@subsubheading Return Values

Return values are passed on the stack:

@example
; COMPUTE Result = CalculateValue()
; Generates:
        .byte #x01, <CalculateValue   ; Push function address
        .byte #x06                    ; EXECUTE (result on stack)
        .byte #x01, <Result           ; Push result address
        .byte #x03                    ; SETWORD (store result)
@end example

@subsubheading Integration with Game Engine

The Forth interpreter integrates with the Phantasia game engine:

@itemize @bullet
@item @strong{Script Loading}: Scripts are loaded into memory at a known address
@item @strong{Cursor Setup}: @code{ForthCursor} is set to point to the script
@item @strong{Execution}: @code{ForthInterpreter} runs the script
@item @strong{Completion}: When @code{ForthEOL} is encountered, control returns
@item @strong{Fault Handling}: Underflow and other errors call @code{.LogFault}
@end itemize

@example
; C entry point for script execution
void RunFountainScript(const uint8_t* script) {
    ForthCursor = script;
    ForthInterpreter();
    /* Control returns here when script completes */
}
@end example

@subsubheading Memory Considerations

When integrating the Forth VM into your system:

@itemize @bullet
@item Reserve RAM for the parameter stack (64-256 bytes recommended)
@item Ensure bytecode memory is readable
@item Provide callable entry points for native functions
@item Implement fault handlers for underflow/overflow
@item Consider watchdog timer for long-running scripts
@end itemize

@subsubheading Example: Complete Script

A complete dialogue script compiled to Forth bytecode:

@example
; MethodShowDialogue - Generated by Eightbol
; Shows dialogue and returns
;
; COBOL source:
;   PERFORM ShowDialogue.
;   IF DialogueComplete THEN
;       PERFORM HandleComplete.
;   ENDIF.
;
MethodShowDialogue:
        .byte #x01, <ShowDialogue     ; Push routine address
        .byte #x06                    ; EXECUTE
        .byte #x01, <DialogueComplete ; Push variable address
        .byte #x05                    ; GETWORD
        .byte #x09                    ; UNLESS (branch if false)
        .byte #x00, #x06              ; Offset: +6 bytes
        .byte #x01, <HandleComplete   ; Push routine address
        .byte #x06                    ; EXECUTE
        .byte #x00                    ; EOL (end of method)
@end example

@subsection Identifier Normalization

The front-end converts identifiers to the canonical PascalCase form (or language-specific
form) before AST production.

